      "claim": "The closest analogue, compound 6p, shares the isopropyl-triazole-biaryl core and showed good brain permeability in mice with brain/plasma ratios of 1.5–2.1, directly supporting meaningful CNS access for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "AZ32, a related heteroaromatic biaryl, was confirmed as an orally bioavailable and BBB-penetrable ATM inhibitor with a favorable free brain-to-plasma ratio, supporting CNS access for this compound class.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "The biaryl tracer BF-158 showed high brain uptake in mice (11.3% ID/g at 2 min), providing additional in vivo evidence that related biaryl heterocycles can penetrate the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Pass: the closest in vivo analogue (C01) demonstrates good brain/plasma exposure in the same triazole-biaryl series, and multiple related heteroaromatic CNS-penetrants support meaningful access; the low-confidence physicochemical prior is outweighed by direct experimental evidence.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct brain/plasma or CSF measurement for the exact query molecule.",
    "Morgan similarities are moderate-to-low; the closest analogue differs by pyridine and benzoxadiazole substituents that may alter permeability or efflux.",
    "Efflux transporter status (P-gp/BCRP) of the query was not experimentally assessed.",
    "The benzoxadiazole moiety may have unique pharmacokinetic properties not captured by the analogues."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

